[Black "Chapter Summary: Key Takeaways"]
[Result "*"]

 { Below is an overview of concepts introduced in this chapter: (1) Types of piece passivity: Piece activity, or lack of it, is always an important feature in any chess position. While short-term passivity is often not serious, we should be extremely careful about medium-term or longer types of passivity. It also matters greatly which piece is passive. We might be able to survive with a permanently passive bishop, while a permanently passive queen is almost always fatal. (2) Generating piece activity: There are various concepts and methods that you can rely on in order to increase your piece activity or reduce that of your opponent’s. Popular techniques include finding ways to paralyse your opponent, sacrificing for piece activity, and piece maneuvers. (3) Piece exchanges: Many factors influence the decision to go for (or avoid) a piece exchange. These include the fight for space, king safety, and the effect that an exchange will have on the pawn structure. (4) Minor piece battles: Here we took a look at situations where the knight tends to be better than a bishop (e.g. when it occupies an outpost) as well as situations where the bishop wins (e.g. play across both flanks) . We also touched upon the value of the two bishops, and the importance of staying alert so that your opponent cannot win the bishop pair for free. (5) Positional sacrifices: As in the case of piece exchanges, there are many reasons why it may be a good idea to sacrifice material. Examples of this include piece activity, opening lines, targeting the king, and improving the structure. Pawn and piece sacrifices are common, but perhaps most common of all is the exchange sacrifice. (6) Dealing with uncertainty: We also briefly discussed a framework for making decisions when you are uncertain of the outcome. By mentally estimating your chances of success in one line, you can determine if it’s a good idea to take a calculated risk, even when you have not worked out a given variation until the end. } 1. { -KEY- }  
 *

