 { White's knight on c3 is under fire. Find the best move. } 17. { -KEY- } Nd5 $1 ( 17. Nb5 $6 { is perhaps still slightly better for White, but much less clear since after } axb5 18. axb5 Rb8 19. b6 Qd7 $13 { White's passed pawn on b6 may be very strong, but it's hard to find a clear way to break through. } ) 
cxd5 18. exd5 $1 { Correct! The connected passers on c5 and d5 are a serious problem for Black. } f5 $6 19. d6 Qc6 20. Bb5 $1 { A beautiful second piece sacrifice! } axb5 21. axb5 Qxb5 ( 21... Qb7 { Hanging on to the material was also losing. } 22. c6 Qb8 23. b6 $1 { It's not every day you see three pawns on the sixth rank. Black would have been hopeless here. For example: } Nf6 24. Ra7 Rxa7 25. bxa7 Qa8 26. Qxb4 Ne6 27. Qb8 { White will promote his a-pawn. } ) 
22. Rxa8 Qc6 23. Rfa1 $18 { White was completely winning in the game Serper,G - Ioannis,N (1993). The rest of the game is included without annotations, in case you are curious to see how it played out. } f4 24. R1a7 Nd7 25. Rxc8+ Qxc8 26. Qd5 fxe3 27. Qe6+ Kf8 28. Rxd7 exf2+ 29. Kf1 Qe8 30. Rf7+ Qxf7 31. Qc8+ Qe8 32. d7 Kf7 33. dxe8=Q+ Rxe8 34. Qb7+ Re7 35. c6 e4 36. c7 e3 37. Qd5+ Kf6 38. Qd6+ Kf7 39. Qd5+ Kf6 40. Qd6+ Kf7 41. Qxe7+ Kxe7 42. c8=Q Bh6 43. Qc5+ Ke8 44. Qb5+ Kd8 45. Qb6+ Kd7 46. Qxg6 e2+ 47. Kxf2 Be3+ 48. Ke1 { Black resigned. } 
