 ( { Should White play } 1. Rxa6 { , or is the more conservative plan of } 1. Ra5 { followed by 2.b5 a stronger choice? } ) 
1. { -KEY- } Rxa6 $1 ( 1. Ra5 { is not a bad move, but after } Bf1 $1 { things get messy, since Black is now ready to play ...a6 next, while after } 2. b5 cxb5 3. Nxb5 Bxb5 4. Rxb5 Kc7 $14 { White stands better due to the space advantage and more active pieces, but Black's position is compact so a breakthrough will not be easy. } ) 
bxa6 2. Rxa6 $18 { Correct! Sacrifices on a6 are not uncommon in such structures, and are typically made very strong by the fact that White has two pawns on the b-file. The reason for this is that White can play b4-b5, while still having another b-pawn to set up a pawn storm. Besides this, Black's a7 and c6 pawns are fixed and so a constant problem for Black. This type of position is generally winning for White, and here it is no exception. If you are unsure about this evaluation, and you sometimes play these queen's gambit structures, it might be fun to analyse from here with an engine. } 
